[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Missing [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m C export (in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner dynamically loads the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m from the shared library. The C code uses [38;5;10m#define gen_message_random SPX_NAMESPACE(gen_message_random)[0m which expands to [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m. The Rust translation had the internal function [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m but never exported it under the [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m name.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Added an [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern "C" fn SPX_gen_message_random(...)[0m wrapper in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m that calls the internal [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m. The output buffer was sized to [38;5;10mSPX_BLAKE512_OUTPUT_BYTES[0m (64) since [38;5;10mblake512_final[0m writes a full 64-byte digest.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Incorrect [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m calls (in [38;5;10mhash_blake.rs[0m)[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is the subtle semantic bug. The C [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m function takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m (it internally does [38;5;10mdatalen >> 3[0m to get byte counts). The C [38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m passes raw byte counts directly:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);     // SPX_N = 24 (treated as 24 bits = 3 bytes)
blakeX_update(&S, optrand, SPX_N);    // same
blakeX_update(&S, m, mlen);           // mlen in bytes, treated as bits
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translator "helpfully" multiplied by 8 to convert bytes to bits:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mblake512_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N], (SPX_N as u64) * 8);  // 192 bits = 24 bytes — WRONG
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
But the C code intentionally passes byte counts to the bit-expecting function, so only [38;5;10mSPX_N / 8 = 3[0m bytes are actually processed per update call. The translator's "correction" changed the hash computation entirely.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Removed the [38;5;10m* 8[0m multiplier so the Rust code passes the same raw values as the C code:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mblake512_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N], SPX_N as u64);  // matches C behavior
